  "live_records": {
    "q-105629": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105629",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$ What is the first component of $f?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
