    "q-105628": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105628",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$ What is the second component of $f?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $6$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =6.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
