    "q-105676": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105676",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{49}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=7Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,4Y,7Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
