    },
    "q-105680": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105680",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.$ What is the first component of $f?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}X,\\,y=5Y,\\,z=2Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3}X,5Y,2Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
