      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r,\\,\\text{_____}⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${y}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}.$ What is the third component of $f?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, substituting\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=3Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get the cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation\n\n$$\nZ=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,\\,Z,\\,3Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r,3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r,\\,3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
